<h2 id="org70d3eb4">UNIX Way</h2>
<div id="text-org70d3eb4" class="outline-text-2">
<p>I’ve never been a big fan of the <code>mkdir</code> (make directory) command in Linux. I like what it does, but it’s a bit too long. It’s not as sleek as the <code>rm</code>, <code>mv</code>, and <code>cp</code> commands. Nevertheless, it perfectly does what it needs to do—make directories.</p>
<div class="org-src-container">
<pre class="src src-sh">mkdir new_directory
mkdir -p path/to/nested/directory
</pre>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div id="outline-container-orgdaae607" class="outline-2">
<h2 id="orgdaae607">Emacs Way</h2>
<div id="text-orgdaae607" class="outline-text-2">
<p>In this instance, I do prefer the Emacs way. All you need to do is open up dired by your preferred method, and press the <code>+</code> sign. Easy. Now you have a new directory.</p>
<p>You also have an interactive <code>M-x make-directory</code> (or <code>M-x mkdir</code>) function if you prefer that.</p>
<p>Likewise, if you have to create new file with a directory that does exist, Emacs will prompt you to create that directory.</p>
